--- a/docs/mycelic/INGESTION.md
+++ b/docs/mycelic/INGESTION.md
@@ -101,6 +101,43 @@ reference while building.
 >   hit@10 within 0.01 of the single store at ~0.64-0.67 agreement. Sharded queries are 2-3x slower at p50 (shards are
 >   searched in turn). On these numbers the default merge is now `channel` (DECISIONS D17); `rrf` remains selectable.
 
+> **Implementation status (ingestion dispositions, 2026-10-09; repairs from the runtime trace, probes e and f).**
+> * **Rejected input has a durable disposition.** Holder migration `0004_dispositions.sql` adds `ingest_rejections`
+>   (`rejection_id, connector_id, source_id, locator, raw_sha256, stage, reason, detail, first_seen_at, last_seen_at, count`),
+>   written in the same transaction that moves the cursor past the item (`IngestQueue.commit_page(extra_sync=...)`). It holds an
+>   unparseable or non-object item (`normalize/normalize_failed`, `locator = line:N` of a JSONL file or `record:N` of a JSON
+>   file), a connector exception (`normalize/<code or class>`), an event that fails the contract or names another holder,
+>   connection or namespace (`validate/invalid:<problems>`), and an admission refusal nobody asked for (`admit/foreign_source`,
+>   `admit/quota`). It never holds text: `raw_sha256` is the hash of the raw line (unparseable lines) or of the item's canonical
+>   JSON, `detail` is the message of a `ConnectorError` only (a foreign exception is recorded by class). The same item read again
+>   raises `count`; a changed item is another row; each connector keeps at most 5000 rows (least recently seen go first).
+>   Not rejections, because they already are dispositions or owner choices: `duplicate`, `historical`, `late_after_delete` (in
+>   `applied_events`), owner exclusion rules, sticky tombstones and no-op inferred deletions (counted in `SyncReport.excluded`).
+>   `SyncReport` gains `rejected`, `rejected_by_reason` and `time_inferred`; `connectors.health` is written after every sync
+>   (`status: degraded`, `reason: rejected_items` when that sync refused input, else `ok`, plus `rejections_on_record`); the holder
+>   stats heartbeat carries `ingest_rejections` (a count); `IngestService.rejections(connector_id)` lists the ledger to the owner.
+>   A second sync that refuses nothing reports `ok` again, but the ledger keeps the rows (`rejections_on_record`).
+> * **Undated records are kept and marked, not presented as fresh (D2).** A content record with no usable `created_at` /
+>   `updated_at` (absent or unparsable) is still ingested, flagged `time_inferred` in `ingest_records.flags` (carried to the export as
+>   `meta.time_inferred = true` and `meta.time_basis`). Its `observed_at` (evidence time) is no longer the ingest time: it is the
+>   source's last-known content time when the connector has one (`local_export`: the header's `content_time` / `updated_at` /
+>   `modified_at` / `exported_at`, else the newest dated record of the same file; `meta.time_basis = "source"`), else the ingest time
+>   with the extra flag `time_ingest_fallback` (`meta.time_basis = "ingest"`). The source time is an upper bound for an undated
+>   record, never evidence that it is that recent; an `order_key` for ordering edits still comes from what the connector saw.
+> * **Explicit forward / share links decide the root, with or without commentary (D3, I4 / §4.5 rule 1).** A `derived_from` link
+>   (`forward | share | crosspost | copy | moved_from | bot_relay`) that names an object (`source_object_id`, or an RFC 822
+>   Message-ID; a missing app, account or type means the record's own) gives the record `origin_key`, the same one-way
+>   `object_key` hash the original has, stored in `ingest_records.origin_key`, and `root_method = explicit`: the root is
+>   the original's own root when that record is held by this holder (resolved when the record is written), otherwise
+>   `origin_root(origin_key)`, a hash of the origin's identity. The export's `meta.object_key` is the `origin_key`, which is what
+>   `knowledge/support.py` merges roots on, so the original (wherever it was ingested), every verbatim forward and every
+>   forward with the forwarder's own words are one independent root. Limits: the merge with the original needs the link to use the
+>   original's identity space (same app label, account and object type; an email forward by Message-ID merges with other forwards
+>   of that email, not with a Gmail record whose id is a Gmail message id); a forward with the marker and a verbatim copy
+>   without it of an original held nowhere still count as two roots; a retelling in own words with no link stays independent
+>   (undetectable by design, probe f4); records ingested before holder migration 0004 have no `origin_key` and keep their content
+>   roots until they are re-ingested.
+
